<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>First pregnancy at 38, at 7 weeks 1 day by today&apos;s dating scan, with moderate nausea and vomiting for a week and early weight loss. Please advise on the plan if it worsens, on aneuploidy screening and diagnostic options at her age, on preeclampsia prevention, and on her tuberculosis risk. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Soon, within one to two weeks; same-day review here if she cannot keep fluids down. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What escalation plan do you advise if her nausea and vomiting worsens, including the thresholds for intravenous fluids or admission and the place of ondansetron before 10 weeks?</item><item>What aneuploidy screening and diagnostic options should we offer at 38, and when?</item><item>Does she need low-dose aspirin for preeclampsia prevention, and from when?</item><item>How should we interpret and act on her family history of tuberculosis in pregnancy?</item><item>What else should the plan for the rest of this pregnancy include because of her age?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Brontë is seen at the request of Dr. Thwaite at 8 weeks 1 day. Nausea has worsened over the week despite doxylamine-pyridoxine two tablets at bedtime: she vomits four or five times a day, keeps down small amounts of fluid and dry food most days, and feels lightheaded only on standing quickly. PUQE-24 score 11. She has had no pain, bleeding, fever, or diarrhea, and no palpitations or tremor. This is a planned first pregnancy; she has no previous pregnancies, no chronic illness, and no thyroid disease. Her elder sisters died of tuberculosis in childhood and two other sisters as young adults, in her household; she has no cough, night sweats, or fever. She is anxious about carrying a pregnancy at her age after losing her mother and sisters young, and wants clear information. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Charlotte Brontë was born on 21 April 1816 in Thornton, Yorkshire, the third of six children of Maria Branwell and her husband, an Anglican clergyman. [D]</item><item>Jane Eyre was first published in 1847 under the pseudonym Currer Bell. [D]</item><item>Her mother died on 15 September 1821, in the 39th year of her age, after a long and painful illness. [D]</item><item>Her mother&apos;s illness may have been cancer; one reference work says uterine cancer. The cause is a later interpretation. [R]</item><item>Her two elder sisters, Maria and Elizabeth, fell ill at the Clergy Daughters&apos; School at Cowan Bridge and died at home in 1825, Maria on 6 May aged 11 and Elizabeth on 15 June aged 10; reference works give tuberculosis. [D]</item><item>Her brother Branwell died on 24 September 1848, aged 31, of several conditions made worse by heavy drinking; tuberculosis is listed among them. [D]</item><item>Her sisters Emily and Anne died of tuberculosis, Emily on 19 December 1848 aged 30 and Anne on 28 May 1849 aged 29. [D]</item><item>On 29 June 1854 she married Arthur Bell Nicholls, her father&apos;s curate, at Haworth. [D]</item><item>In late November 1854, after a long walk on the moors to a waterfall that ended in heavy rain, she began to shiver and had a bad lingering sore throat and cold that made her thin and weak. [D]</item><item>Early in 1855, after a visit to Gawthorpe and a long walk over damp ground in thin shoes, she was attacked by &quot;new sensations of perpetual nausea, and ever-recurring faintness&quot;; a doctor who was called assigned a natural cause for her indisposition. [D]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

