<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Nausea and vomiting of pregnancy, moderate and worsening (O21.9). PUQE-24 score 11 with 4.3 percent weight loss and normal electrolytes; not yet hyperemesis gravidarum, but at risk of it. Severity is judged by the PUQE-24 score, weight, hydration, and electrolytes; urine ketones are not a measure of dehydration. [S]</item><item>First pregnancy at 38 (O09.519). A singleton pregnancy at an advanced maternal age with no other risk factor yet identified. Two moderate preeclampsia risk factors (nulliparity and age 35 or older). The negative interferon-gamma release assay, with no symptoms, excludes latent tuberculosis infection for practical purposes; old household exposure needs no further testing. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Today&apos;s weight (44.0 kg, 4.3 percent below her pre-pregnancy weight), vital signs, and metabolic panel (normal, potassium 3.6 mmol/L); the dating scan; the first prenatal tests (blood group O RhD positive, antibody screen negative, rubella immune, hepatitis B, HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis C negative, urine culture no growth); the interferon-gamma release assay (negative); PHQ-9 5. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Raise doxylamine-pyridoxine to four tablets a day now and add metoclopramide 10 mg three times a day before meals, stopping it at once if restlessness or abnormal movements occur. [S]</item><item>Ondansetron is a reasonable next step even before 10 weeks if these fail: counsel her that the absolute increase in orofacial clefting is very small and must be weighed against uncontrolled vomiting; check potassium, magnesium, and the QTc first if electrolytes are abnormal. [S]</item><item>Same-day assessment, with admission or day-unit care, if she cannot keep fluids down for 24 hours, loses more than 5 percent of her pre-pregnancy weight, is orthostatic, or has electrolyte abnormalities. Then give 0.9% saline with potassium guided by daily electrolytes, thiamine before any dextrose, magnesium and phosphate replacement with refeeding precautions, and weight-adjusted low-molecular-weight heparin while admitted; check TSH, free T4, and liver tests, expecting a suppressed TSH from hCG that needs no antithyroid drug. [S]</item><item>Offer cell-free DNA screening from 10 weeks with a nuchal translucency scan at 11 to 14 weeks, or diagnostic testing by chorionic villus sampling from 10 weeks or amniocentesis from 15 weeks; she prefers screening, with diagnostic testing only after a positive screen. [S]</item><item>Start aspirin 81 mg at bedtime between 12 and 16 weeks and continue to delivery. [S]</item><item>No further tuberculosis testing is needed; a chest radiograph with shielding only if symptoms develop. [S]</item><item>Detailed anatomy scan at 18 to 22 weeks; mood screening each trimester and after any admission; standard glucose screening at 24 to 28 weeks; in the third trimester, a growth scan and a discussion of antenatal fetal surveillance and the timing of delivery. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Thwaite the same day; Ms. Brontë agrees with the plan. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary obstetric care continues with Dr. Thwaite. Maternal-fetal medicine will see her again for the anatomy scan, sooner if a screen is positive or hyperemesis needs admission. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
